WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.220
Detection engineering metrics should support decision-making.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:07.180
Useful measures may include alert quality, analyst feedback, detection coverage,

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:11.435
investigation outcomes, time to tune detections, and reduction in unnecessary noise.

00:00:11.435 --> 00:00:13.240
Vanity metrics can distort behavior.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.985
Counting total alerts, dashboards,

00:00:14.985 --> 00:00:18.900
or rules may reward activity without showing whether the program is helping defenders make

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.165
better decisions.

00:00:20.165 --> 00:00:22.990
Coverage metrics are useful when they are grounded in real scope.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:27.210
Teams can map detections to relevant threat behaviors, critical systems, data sources,

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:31.000
or control objectives, while still noting assumptions and visibility gaps.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.990
Success should be measured by outcomes and learning.

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:37.290
A mature program uses metrics to decide where to invest, which detections to tune,

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.500
which gaps to close, and how to improve analyst experience.
